The invariant — balance never goes negative — is enforced in exactly one place, inside `withdraw`, because there's no other way to change `balance`:

```java
class BankAccount {
    private double balance;

    BankAccount(double openingBalance) {
        if (openingBalance < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("negative opening balance");
        this.balance = openingBalance;
    }

    void withdraw(double amount) {
        if (amount > balance) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("insufficient funds");
        }
        balance -= amount;
    }

    double getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
}
```

There's deliberately no `setBalance()`. Every legal way to change `balance` — the constructor, `withdraw`, and (not shown) `deposit` — funnels through code that can enforce the rule; a caller has no path around it.
